---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../site';

// Answers are HTML strings so they can carry links. Keep them short and true.
const groups = [
  {
    title: 'The basics',
    items: [
      {
        q: 'What is Drover?',
        a: `One interface above your native coding harnesses. A hub and a daemon on each host run sessions and record them. You control them from an iOS app, a web cockpit, an HTTP API and an MCP server. Read the <a href="${LINKS.article}" rel="noopener">launch article</a>.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'What is Drover not?',
        a: `Drover is not a replacement harness, a model or a hosted service. It does not select the model for you. It has no user accounts.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'How does Drover relate to an orchestrator such as OpenClaw or Hermes?',
        a: `The orchestrator plans the work. Drover runs the sessions and keeps the history. Drover also collects OpenClaw and Hermes activity, but it does not control them. See the <a href="/use-cases/#orchestrator">orchestrator use case</a>.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'How does Drover differ from Herdr or Claude Code remote control?',
        a: `They are good tools for different parts of the problem. See the <a href="/#how-it-differs">comparison on the home page</a>.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Which harnesses can Drover drive?',
        a: `Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity (agy) and DeepSeek Harness, and a shell terminal. A harness is available on a host when its CLI is installed there.`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Cost and license',
    items: [
      {
        q: 'What does Drover cost?',
        a: `Nothing. You bring your own harnesses, model accounts and API keys.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'What is the license?',
        a: `<a href="${LINKS.license}" rel="noopener">Apache-2.0</a>.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Is the iOS app free?',
        a: `Yes. You build it from source with Xcode. It is not on the App Store. A public beta is coming soon.`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Data and privacy',
    items: [
      {
        q: 'Where is my data stored?',
        a: `On your machines: in <code>~/.drover/</code>, in your PostgreSQL database and, if you select it, in a DuckLake lake. There is no Drover cloud and no Drover account.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Does data leave my machines?',
        a: `Only through integrations you configure or already use. Examples are a model provider for summaries, Apple push notifications and the update check on GitHub. See <a href="/docs/security-privacy/#when-data-leaves-your-machines">Security and privacy</a>.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Can summaries stay local?',
        a: `No. Summaries use the Claude Code CLI or the Anthropic API, and both send transcript text to Anthropic. Embeddings can use a local Ollama backend. Drover works without summaries.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Can I expose Drover to the internet?',
        a: `No. Use localhost, a private LAN or a private Tailscale network. The listeners do not provide TLS. A device or host credential gives shell access to your hosts.`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Platforms and behavior',
    items: [
      {
        q: 'Which platforms does Drover support?',
        a: `The hub and the host daemon run on macOS and Linux with Python 3.11 or later. The app needs iOS 18 or later. Windows is not supported.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Do I need PostgreSQL?',
        a: `Yes, for a new hub. Use your own PostgreSQL, or let the installer manage a local container. Existing single-machine installs can stay on DuckDB.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'What happens when I am offline?',
        a: `The hub, the hosts and the phone communicate on your private network. Your agents still need their model providers. If a host disconnects, the hub holds a stop request until the host confirms it.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'What happens when the hub restarts?',
        a: `The host daemons own the agent processes, so the sessions continue. The history is already in the control store. Pairing codes that you did not use become invalid.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'How mature is Drover?',
        a: `Drover is for technical users and its releases are frequent. Read the <a href="${LINKS.changelog}" rel="noopener">changelog</a> before you upgrade. See the known gaps on the <a href="/features/#roadmap">roadmap</a>.`,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="FAQ" description="What Drover is, what it costs, where your data is stored and which platforms it supports.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
      <h1>Questions and answers.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        For other questions, open an issue on <a href={LINKS.issues} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      {
        groups.map((g) => (
          <div class="group">
            <h2>{g.title}</h2>
            {g.items.map((item) => (
              <details>
                <summary>{item.q}</summary>
                <p set:html={item.a} />
              </details>
            ))}
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .group {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  .group h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  details {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-m);
    background: var(--surface);
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  details[open] {
    border-color: var(--line-strong);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow);
  }
  summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
  summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }
  summary::after {
    content: '+';
    flex: none;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--ember);
    transition: transform 0.2s ease;
  }
  details[open] summary::after {
    transform: rotate(45deg);
  }
  details p {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0 1.2rem 1.1rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }
</style>
